.nh
.SH NAME
ktm-guide-operators \- Operators (chapter 4 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Arithmetic, bit operations, assignment, comparisons and logic, ktm's chained comparisons, \fBsizeof\fR and \fBtypeof\fR, casts, and the order operators apply in.
.SH Arithmetic
.PP
\fB+\fR, \fB\-\fR, \fB*\fR, \fB/\fR and \fB%\fR work as in C. Integer division drops the fraction; with a float on either side the result is a float.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 a = 7, b = 2;
    printf("%d %d %d %d %d\en", a + b, a \- b, a * b, a / b, a % b);
    printf("%.2f %.2f\en", 7.0 / 2, a / 2.0);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
9 5 14 3 1
3.50 3.50
.EE
.RE
.PP
There is no exponent operator: use \fBmath.power(x, y)\fR, which takes and returns an \fBf64\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import math;

void main() {
    printf("%.0f %.3f\en", math.power(2, 10), math.power(2, 0.5));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1024 1.414
.EE
.RE
.SS Unary minus and plus
.PP
Unary \fB\-\fR and \fB+\fR take a number. A narrower integer is widened to \fBi64\fR first, so negating a \fBu8\fR gives a negative number, not a wrapped byte.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 small = 200;
    i64 n = 5;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", \-small, \-n, +n);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
\-200 \-5 5
.EE
.RE
.SH Bit operations
.PP
\fB&\fR (and), \fB|\fR (or), \fB^\fR (exclusive or), \fB~\fR (not), \fB<<\fR and \fB>>\fR (shifts).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 a = 0b1100, b = 0b1010;
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", a & b, a | b, a ^ b, ~a);
    printf("%d %d\en", 1 << 10, 1024 >> 3);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
8 14 6 \-13
1024 128
.EE
.RE
.PP
Flags are the usual use: one bit per option, \fB|\fR to combine, \fB&\fR to test, \fB& ~\fR to clear.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
const i64 READ = 1 << 0;
const i64 WRITE = 1 << 1;
const i64 EXEC = 1 << 2;

void show(i64 mode) {
    printf("%c%c%c\en", mode & READ ? 'r' : '\-', mode & WRITE ? 'w' : '\-', mode & EXEC ? 'x' : '\-');
}

void main() {
    i64 mode = READ | WRITE;
    show(mode);
    mode |= EXEC;
    show(mode);
    mode &= ~WRITE;
    show(mode);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
rw\-
rwx
r\-x
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB>>\fR on a signed value keeps the sign (an arithmetic shift); on an unsigned value it shifts in zeros.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 neg = \-16;
    u64 big = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
    printf("%d %u\en", neg >> 2, big >> 60);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
\-4 15
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB~\fR takes an integer only: \fB~\fR on an \fBf64\fR is an error.
.SH Assignment
.PP
\fB=\fR stores a value; \fB+=\fR, \fB\-=\fR, \fB*=\fR, \fB/=\fR, \fB%=\fR, \fB&=\fR, \fB|=\fR, \fB^=\fR, \fB<<=\fR and \fB>>=\fR combine an operation with it. \fB++\fR and \fB\-\-\fR add or subtract one, before (\fB++n\fR) or after (\fBn++\fR) the value is used.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 10;
    n += 5;
    n *= 2;
    n \-= 1;
    n /= 3;
    n %= 4;
    printf("%d\en", n);

    i64 i = 5;
    i64 before = i++;
    i64 after = ++i;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", before, after, i);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1
5 7 7
.EE
.RE
.PP
An assignment is also an expression: its value is what was stored. That value has the type of the left side, so it is cut to fit:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 a, b, c;
    a = b = c = 7;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", a, b, c);

    u8 byte;
    i64 v = (byte = 300);
    printf("%d\en", v);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7 7 7
44
.EE
.RE
.SH Comparisons
.PP
\fB==\fR, \fB!=\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB<=\fR, \fB>\fR and \fB>=\fR give 1 or 0.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 a = 3, b = 5;
    printf("%d %d %d %d %d %d\en", a == b, a != b, a < b, a <= b, a > b, a >= b);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 1 1 1 0 0
.EE
.RE
.PP
On \fBu64\fR values, comparisons, \fB/\fR, \fB%\fR and \fB>>\fR are unsigned:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u64 big = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
    i64 neg = \-1;
    printf("%d %d\en", big > 1, neg > 1);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 0
.EE
.RE
.SS Chained comparisons
.PP
As in mathematics, \fBlo < x < hi\fR means \fBlo < x && x < hi\fR, and the middle value is computed once.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bool inRange(i64 x) { return 1 <= x <= 10; }

void main() {
    printf("%d %d %d\en", inRange(0), inRange(5), inRange(10));
    i64 a = 1, b = 2, c = 3;
    printf("%d %d\en", a < b < c, a < c < b);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 1 1
1 0
.EE
.RE
.SH Logic
.PP
\fB&&\fR (and), \fB||\fR (or) and \fB!\fR (not) take any number or pointer: zero and \fBnull\fR are false, everything else is true. They give 1 or 0.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    f64 half = 0.5;
    u8 *none = null;
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", half && 1, !half, none || 0, !none);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 0 0 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB&&\fR and \fB||\fR stop as soon as the answer is known: the right side runs only when it is needed. That makes "check, then use" safe in one expression.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bool loud(u8 *name, bool v) {
    printf("%s ", name);
    return v;
}

void main() {
    bool r1 = loud("a", false) && loud("b", true);
    printf("\en");
    bool r2 = loud("c", true) || loud("d", true);
    printf("\en%d %d\en", r1, r2);

    u8 *name = null;
    if (name && name[0] == 'x') {
        printf("starts with x\en");
    } else {
        printf("no name, and no crash\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
a 
c 
0 1
no name, and no crash
.EE
.RE
.SH The conditional operator
.PP
\fBcond ? a : b\fR is \fBa\fR when \fBcond\fR is true, else \fBb\fR\&. Only the chosen side runs.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 abs(i64 n) { return n < 0 ? \-n : n; }

void main() {
    i64 count = 1;
    printf("%d %d\en", abs(\-4), abs(4));
    printf("%d item%s\en", count, count == 1 ? "" : "s");
    count = 3;
    printf("%d item%s\en", count, count == 1 ? "" : "s");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4 4
1 item
3 items
.EE
.RE
.SH sizeof, alignof and typeof
.PP
\fBsizeof\fR is the size of a type or an expression, \fBalignof\fR its alignment, in bytes. \fBtypeof\fR is the name of an expression's type, as a string.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Pair { i32 a; i64 b; };

void main() {
    i64 arr[10];
    printf("%d %d\en", sizeof(arr), sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]));
    printf("%d %d\en", sizeof(Pair), alignof(Pair));
    printf("%s | %s | %s\en", typeof(1 + 1), typeof(&arr[0]), typeof(arr));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
80 10
16 8
i64 | i64 * | i64[10]
.EE
.RE
.PP
All three are worked out at compile time. Their operand never runs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 noisy() {
    printf("called!\en");
    return 1;
}

void main() {
    printf("%s %d\en", typeof(noisy()), sizeof(noisy()));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i64 8
.EE
.RE
.SH Casts
.PP
\fB(type)value\fR converts a value, as in C. A cast binds tighter than any binary operator: \fB(f64)a / b\fR converts \fBa\fR, then divides.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    f64 f = 3.9;
    i64 i = (i64)f;
    printf("%d\en", i);
    printf("%.1f %.1f\en", (f64)(7 / 2), (f64)7 / 2);
    printf("%.1f\en", (f64)\-i / 2);

    u8 *s = "hey";
    i64 address = (i64)s;
    printf("%c\en", *(u8 *)address);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3
3.0 3.5
\-1.5
h
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A cast applies to the whole postfix expression after it: \fB(i64)p.n\fR casts \fBp.n\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Any type works, function types included: \fB(fn(i64) i64)ptr\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Types and values (see ktm-guide-types-and-values(7)) covers what each conversion does to the value.
.SH Precedence
.PP
The same as C, from the tightest to the loosest:
.PP
